  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 105 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nTuesday will once again bring record-challenging temperatures as\nthe ridge continues to build over the region. The NBM\ninterquartile spread only shows a 4-degree difference between\nthe 25th to 75th percentiles, leading to fairly high confidence\nin temperatures surpassing 90 degrees for the entirety of the\nforecast area. This will likely be the hottest day in the\nforecast period, with records at all climate sites in jeopardy. \n\nThe previously mentioned closed low and ingested tropical\nsystems will continue over the desert southwest on Tuesday, with\nupper level energy and moisture content beginning to filter \ninto the area ahead of the trough by Tuesday evening. Despite\ncontinued discrepancies between ensembles, there's been an\nincrease in both the precipitation chances and the QPF\nprobabilities since previous forecasts. A cold front is progged\nto drop south into the area late Tuesday into Wednesday before \nstalling out and lifting northeast on Thursday ahead of a\nsecondary cold front that sweeps through Thursday night into\nFriday. If this scenario were to play out, we'd see a prolonged\nperiod of rainfall spanning across multiple days. Current NBM \n24-hour QPF probabilities showcase the following:\n\n> 1\": 55-85%\n> 2\": 35-65%\n> 3\": <30%\n\nwith rain chances increasing Tuesday night (20-50%), peaking on\nWednesday (85-95%), continuing on Thursday (60-80%), before\ndecreasing Thursday night (20-40%). That being said, there is a\nscenario where the system stalls out to the west, effectively \ncanceling out rain chances for some locations. So the \npositioning of this system and how it evolves will be put under \nintense scrutiny over the next few days until we get a better \nidea of how it will evolve. \n\nAnother area we're monitoring is the severe weather chances.\nWith the warm airmass still in place during peak heating on\nTuesday and dewpoints increasing to the upper 60s/low 70s, \ninstability is expected to increase throughout the day. The \nbetter chances of severe weather occurring look to remain to our\nsouthwest over SW KS/western OK/TX panhandle, however as the \nevening progresses and showers/storms begin to push into our \nnorthwest overnight, we could see an isolated severe threat. SPC\nhas introduced a Marginal (Level 1 of 5) risk over the \nnorthwest CWA to account for this potential, so we'll need to \ncontinue monitoring as we get closer to Tuesday. \n\nOnce this system moves through, cooler temperatures will be\nushered into the area next weekend.",
